{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~8–15 €/t), especially from ~day 5 onward. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: when history shows a late upswing/spike (e.g., into low/mid-80s), the model extrapolates or holds levels, but the market mean-reverts and sells off. Add a mean-reversion term that pulls forecasts back toward a medium-term average rather than extending the last move.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following local highs, the model predicts rebounds/new highs (sometimes rising into mid/high-80s) while outcomes continue drifting lower. Cap upside and dampen post-spike rebounds for 2–4 weeks.",
    "Underestimation of drawdown persistence: the model expects stabilization around high-70s/low-80s, but realized paths show a prolonged decline into low-70s/high-60s. Increase downside persistence (momentum) once a downtrend starts.",
    "Horizon-specific error growth: near-term (days 1–3) errors are smaller; medium/long horizon (days 7–30) errors widen and skew upward. Use horizon-weighted bias correction (larger negative adjustment at longer horizons)."
  ]
}
